# Testing

Every compiler pass and VM feature change ships with tests. Arrange–Act–Assert; one behavior per test; `unwrap` is fine inside `#[cfg(test)]`.

## Where tests go

| Kind | Location |
|---|---|
| Unit tests | `#[cfg(test)]` module next to the code under test |
| End-to-end programs | `tests/integration/tests/*.rs` driving full compile/run of Phoenix sources |
| Diagnostics fixtures | `tests/integration/diagnostics/` — `name.phx` with a `name.stderr` sidecar holding the expected rendered diagnostic |
| CLI fixtures | `tests/cli/fixtures/` |

## What to cover, per stage

- **Lexer**: table-driven (`input`, `expected`, `name` per row); one row per token type and edge case (empty input, max length, unterminated literals).
- **Parser**: valid programs assert AST shape; invalid programs assert **error recovery** — the rest of the file still parses and later diagnostics still surface.
- **Typeck**: the core Phoenix guarantees — use-after-move (diagnostic cites the move site), no implicit numeric conversions, branch type unification, Copyable eligibility.
- **Bytecode/verifier**: round-trip encode/decode; malformed modules (bad magic, OOB sections, bad jumps, stack underflow) produce clean errors, never panics.
- **VM**: observable program behavior via integration tests, plus error paths (traps, invalid entry).

Test error paths and edge cases, not just happy paths. When a bug is fixed, add the regression test in the same commit.

Run `just test` for the workspace, `just test-lang` for the CLI/E2E suite (it runs single-threaded by design — keep it that way). New diagnostics need a `.stderr` fixture so rendering changes are caught.
